.Dd August 2, 2026
.Dt GITHUB-TOKEN 1
.Os
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh NAME
.Nm github-token
.Nd supply short-lived GitHub App tokens to Git and the GitHub CLI
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm credential
.Op Cm get | store | erase
.Nm
.Cm gh
.Ar OWNER Ns / Ns Ar REPOSITORY
.Fl -
.Ar gh-argument ...
.Nm
.Cm check
.Ar OWNER Ns / Ns Ar REPOSITORY
.Nm
.Cm version
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
obtains a repository-scoped GitHub App installation token from
.Xr github-token-broker 8
and hands it to Git or to the GitHub CLI.
A token is scoped to one repository, is never written to disk, and is obtained
fresh for each use rather than held.
.Pp
The client holds no configuration file.
It is told where the broker is, and it names one repository per request.
It never learns the App identity, the private key path, or which other
repositories the broker serves.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm credential Op Cm get | store | erase
Serve Git's credential-helper protocol on standard input and output.
Git supplies the operation.
See
.Sx CREDENTIAL HELPER BEHAVIOR .
.It Cm gh Ar OWNER Ns / Ns Ar REPOSITORY Fl - Ar gh-argument ...
Run the real GitHub CLI authenticated for one repository.
The repository is named explicitly rather than inferred from the working
directory, the
.Ql --
separator is required, and at least one argument must follow it.
See
.Sx GITHUB CLI INTEGRATION .
.It Cm check Ar OWNER Ns / Ns Ar REPOSITORY
Report whether the broker is reachable and will serve a repository.
Nothing is minted.
On success it writes the repository and its permission ceiling to standard
output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
example-owner/example-repo actions:read;contents:write
.Ed
.It Cm version
Print the version and exit.
This is answered before the environment is read, so it works on a host where
the broker endpoint is not configured yet.
.El
.Pp
A repository is written as
.Ar OWNER Ns / Ns Ar REPOSITORY ,
spelled the way GitHub spells it and without a
.Pa .git
suffix.
A value that is not already canonical is refused rather than repaired, so no
two spellings can collapse onto one identity.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh GIT INTEGRATION
Configure the helper and path-aware credential matching:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
git config --global credential.helper '!github-token credential'
git config --global \e
    credential.https://github.com.useHttpPath true
export GITHUB_TOKEN_BROKER_ENDPOINT=\e
    unix:///run/github-token-broker/broker.sock
.Ed
.Pp
A bot that clones needs
.Fl -global :
without it,
.Ic git config
must run inside an existing repository, and the one being cloned does not exist
yet.
Drop it to configure a single repository instead.
.Pp
Git resolves a bare helper name as
.Ql git-credential- Ns Ar NAME ,
and runs the value as a command only when it begins with
.Ql \&!
or names an absolute path.
.Pp
Without
.Cm useHttpPath
Git omits the repository path, and one repository's token would satisfy a
request for another.
.Pp
The socket path above is an example.
.Ev GITHUB_TOKEN_BROKER_ENDPOINT
has to name whatever the broker binds; see
.Ic listen
in
.Xr github-token-broker-config 5 .
.Pp
The two
.Ic git config
settings persist; the export does not.
Git spawns the helper as a child, so the variable has to be set wherever Git
runs: a shell profile, a service unit, or a CI job.
Exporting it in one shell configures that shell alone.
.Pp
Use HTTPS remotes with no embedded credentials.
Clone, fetch, and push then work.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh CREDENTIAL HELPER BEHAVIOR
The helper answers
.Cm get
with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
username=x-access-token
password=INSTALLATION_TOKEN
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm store
and
.Cm erase
are answered without minting.
A token lives for an hour and is never persisted, so there is nothing to store
or erase.
.Pp
Declining and failing are different.
Git treats a helper that returns nothing as having no credential and carries on
unauthenticated, which is correct for a repository this broker does not serve.
An unreachable broker is reported instead.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Repository not allowlisted
Declines silently.
Git proceeds unauthenticated.
.It Host or protocol not served
Declines silently.
Only
.Ql https
requests for
.Ql github.com
are served, and Git asks broadly.
.It \&No repository path
Reported on standard error, then declines.
This means
.Cm useHttpPath
is missing.
.It Repository path unusable
Reported on standard error, then declines.
The path is present but is not
.Ar OWNER Ns / Ns Ar REPOSITORY .
.It Broker unreachable
Reported on standard error, and exits non-zero rather than degrading a private
repository into a confusing anonymous failure.
.El
.Pp
All but the last decline, so Git carries on unauthenticated and the failure
surfaces as GitHub's own
.Sy 403
or
.Sy 404 .
When a push or fetch starts failing on permissions, check here first:
.Dl $ github-token check example-owner/example-repo
.Pp
Nothing read from or written to Git is logged; the input can carry a password
on a
.Cm store
operation.
The format is line-based, so a token holding whitespace or a control character
is refused: it could otherwise append fields of its own.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh GITHUB CLI INTEGRATION
.Bd -literal -offset indent
github-token gh example-owner/example-repo -- pr create --fill
.Ed
.Pp
The token is set as
.Ev GH_TOKEN
in the child process only,
.Ev GITHUB_TOKEN
is removed from the child so it cannot take precedence, and
.Ev GH_REPO
pins the child to the repository the token is scoped to.
Arguments are passed individually and never through a shell.
Nothing is written to
.Pa hosts.yml ,
.Cm gh auth login
is never run, and the token disappears when the child exits.
.Pp
One token is minted before the child starts and is never renewed, so a child
still running an hour later holds one GitHub no longer accepts.
Long work belongs in a sequence of
.Cm gh
invocations rather than a single long-lived child.
Git is unaffected, since it spawns the helper once per remote operation.
.Pp
The GitHub CLI is taken from
.Ev GITHUB_TOKEN_BROKER_GH
when set, and otherwise found as
.Ql gh
on
.Ev PATH .
The search skips this executable, so a deployment that puts the wrapper on
.Ev PATH
under the name
.Ql gh
does not make it launch itself.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev GITHUB_TOKEN_BROKER_ENDPOINT
Required.
Where the broker is, as
.Ql unix:///absolute/path/to/broker.sock ,
matching the socket the broker binds.
A query or fragment is refused, as is any other scheme.
.It Ev GITHUB_TOKEN_BROKER_GH
Absolute path to the real GitHub CLI, when it should not be searched for.
.El
.Pp
No credential variable exists.
Write permission on the socket is the authorization, so there is no shared
secret to distribute or rotate.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Following the conventional
.In sysexits.h
values, so a caller can tell an operator mistake from an outage.
.Bl -tag -width "64" -compact
.It Sy 0
Success.
.It Sy 64
The command line or the environment was wrong.
.It Sy 69
The broker could not be reached, or did not answer within 20 seconds.
.It Sy 70
The broker answered but could not serve the request.
Its log holds the reason; see DIAGNOSTICS in
.Xr github-token-broker 8 .
.It Sy 75
The broker was reached but GitHub was not, so retrying can help.
Apart from
.Sy 69 ,
which is the broker itself: here it is running and configured, and GitHub is
what is missing.
.It Sy 77
The broker refused the repository.
.It Sy 78
The broker and GitHub disagree about what it may mint, so retrying cannot help:
the repository is allowlisted but not installed, or GitHub rejected the App
credential.
The broker's log says which; see DIAGNOSTICS in
.Xr github-token-broker 8 .
This is not the broker's own
.Sy 78 ,
which is a configuration document it refused at startup.
.El
.Pp
.Cm gh
instead returns the child's status verbatim, so a value here only ever reflects
a failure that happened before the child started.
.\" -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Sh EXAMPLES
Confirm the broker will serve a repository, without minting anything:
.Dl $ github-token check example-owner/example-repo
.Pp
Clone over HTTPS once the helper is configured:
.Dl $ git clone https://github.com/example-owner/example-repo
.Pp
Open a pull request as the App:
.Dl $ github-token gh example-owner/example-repo \-\- pr create \-\-fill
.Pp
Reach a broker on the host from inside a container through a bind mount.
One socket serves any number of containers at once.
What to mount depends on the runtime, and the right choice keeps a container
reaching a broker that restarts:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Linux
Mount the socket's directory, read-only.
A mounted socket file stays bound to the socket present when the container
started, so it stops answering once the broker restarts;
a mounted directory shows the new socket.
Read-only keeps a container, which runs as root by default, from replacing the
socket every other client connects to; connecting to a socket needs no write
access to the mount.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ docker run \e
    -v /run/github-token-broker:/run/github-token-broker:ro \e
    -e GITHUB_TOKEN_BROKER_ENDPOINT=unix:///run/github-token-broker/broker.sock \e
    my-bot-image
.Ed
.Pp
The mount keeps the host's owner, group, and mode.
A container running as another user needs
.Fl -user
matching the owner, or
.Fl -group-add
against a
.Ql 0660
socket.
.It Docker Desktop
Mount the socket itself.
Its file sharing carries no socket inside a mounted directory, but forwards a
mounted socket by path, so a restarted broker is reached with no change to the
container:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ docker run \e
    -v /path/to/broker.sock:/broker.sock \e
    -e GITHUB_TOKEN_BROKER_ENDPOINT=unix:///broker.sock \e
    my-bot-image
.Ed
.Pp
The file sharing layer re-owns the socket to
.Ql root
inside the container, so a container running as root connects with no further
arrangement.
.El
.Pp
There is no network transport, so a caller that cannot see the host filesystem
has no route to the broker.
